:   9. cite32†A Appendix L43:     1. cite33†A.1 Trace Result of Closed-Sourced Vendor Kernels. L44:     2. cite34†A.2 Per-Vendor Splicing Details. L45:       1. cite35†A.2.1 NVIDIA GPUs. L46:       2. cite36†A.2.2 AMD GPUs. L47: cite37†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L48: \nL49: arXiv:2609.28769v1 [cs.AR] 23 Sep 2026\nL50: # Xtrace: High-Fidelity GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing\nL51: via Binary-Level Instruction Splicing\nL52: \nL53: DOI: cite38†XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX†doi.org Conference: Make sure to enter the correct conference title from your rights confirmation email; June 03–05, 2026; Woodstock, NY ISBN: 978-1-4503-XXXX-X/2026/06\nL54: Zhuobin Huang email: zhuobin@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Kai Zhang email: kai.zhang@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Weihao Cui email: weihao.cui@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Hongshi Tan email: hongshi@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Liang Luo email: liangluo@meta.com Affiliation: Meta, USA , Christopher Dewan email: cmd@meta.com Affiliation: Meta, Japan , Shen Li email: shenli@meta.com Affiliation: Meta, USA and Bingsheng He email: dcsheb@nus.edu.sg Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore\nL55: 2026\nL56: ###### Abstract.\nL57: Modern GPU kernels fuse increasingly more work into a single kernel, and intra-kernel tracing has become the mainstream method to profile them. Tracing inserts probes into the kernel to record its runtime states, and the fidelity of the trace determines the efficiency of performance optimization. Unfortunately, existing tools insert probes before compilation. These tools interfere with the compiler’s optimizations, so they trace a different binary from the one the GPU executes.\nL58: They also add significant runtime overhead.\nL59: Xtrace is the first GPU kernel tracing system with near-zero compile-time interference and minimized runtime overhead. Xtrace inserts probes directly into the compiled kernel binary. It reuses only the registers that hold dead values at the insertion address and resolves all hazards with the compiler’s hazard tables. It further schedules the instruction order, register allocation, and control bits to minimize the runtime overhead the probe introduces.\nL60: Xtrace supports 19 NVIDIA and AMD GPU architectures, and is publicly available for use at cite39†https://g-watch.github.io†g-watch.github.io .\nL61: We evaluate Xtrace on major production large language model (LLM) kernels against the state-of-the-art tracers Neutrino and IKET from NVIDIA. On H100, B300, and MI300X GPUs, Xtrace preserves 94–98% of the instructions of the kernel, while existing tools preserve only 8–48%. Xtrace adds only 0.9–2.8% overhead, while existing tools add 3.8–75.6%. Xtrace guides a coding agent to reach the same FlashAttention-3 performance with 3.9$\\times$ fewer iterations than existing traces do.\nL62: Thanks to our binary-level instrumentation, Xtrace also traces the faster closed-source cuDNN kernel, which guides the agent to lift the open-source FlashAttention-4 by 5.2–13.3% in throughput.\nL63: ## 1. Introduction\nL64: \nL65: (a) Binary similarity.\nL66: \nL67: (b) Tracing overhead.\nL68: \nL69: Figure 1. Two sources of the tracing fidelity loss in Neutrino [cite40†36 ] and IKET [cite41†57 ] across three representative kernels.\nL70: Global investment in AI infrastructure continues to grow rapidly [cite42†29 , cite43†38 ], with GPUs forming a major part of the cost of training and serving large language models (LLMs) [cite44†19 , cite45†60 ]. To improve GPU utilization, modern LLM workloads increasingly rely on fused kernels [cite46†22 , cite47†64 , cite48†74 , cite49†26 , cite50†28 , cite51†25 , cite52†24 , cite53†77 , cite54†3 ], which combine computation, memory accesses, and communication within a single kernel.\nL71: Optimizing such kernels requires accurate timing information to expose instruction overlap, pipeline bubbles, and idle periods [cite55†35 , cite47†64 , cite56†63 , cite57†62 , cite58†16 ]. However, traditional GPU profilers mainly report aggregate statistics from hardware counters [cite59†61 , cite60†56 , cite61†12 ], which are insufficient to reveal these fine-grained timing relationships\nL72: As a fine-grained profiling technique, intra-kernel tracing [cite62†2 , cite63†79 , cite64†31 , cite40†36 , cite41†57 ] has emerged as a mainstream approach. Tracing^{1}^{1} 1 We refer to intra-kernel tracing simply as tracing in the rest of this paper. provides an “X-ray” view of GPU kernel execution at nanosecond resolution. It inserts probes into the kernel to record runtime states, such as precise timestamps from hardware timers, thereby exposing detailed execution behavior at fine temporal granularity.\nL73: Such fine-grained visibility is useful only if tracing preserves the execution it observes. We refer to this property as tracing fidelity, defined as the ability to preserve the original instruction behavior and reveal the true execution time of the probed kernel. However, achieving high fidelity remains challenging. State-of-the-art tracing tools, such as Neutrino [cite40†36 ] and IKET [cite41†57 ], still incur substantial fidelity loss because their instrumentation perturbs the original execution.\nL74: Specifically, we identify two sources of fidelity loss, each arising from a distinct technical challenge:\nL75: First, inserting probe instructions while leaving the original execution unchanged is fundamentally difficult. State-of-the-art tracing methods typically insert probe instructions and reserve additional registers before compilation. These changes interfere with compiler optimization, causing the generated traced binary to differ substantially from the original target binary.\nL76: We quantify this distortion using binary similarity, defined as the fraction of instructions in the traced binary that remain identical to those in the original uninstrumented binary. Figure cite65†1(a) reports the worst case across tracing workloads. Neutrino preserves only 8–20% binary similarity across FlashAttention-3 (FA-3), FlashMLA, and FA-4, while IKET preserves only 13–48%. Such low similarity indicates that their traces characterize an execution different from the one they intend to observe.\nL77: Second, preserving runtime fidelity requires low probe overhead. Existing tracing tools insert probes or reserve probe resources before compilation. These probes require registers to hold profiling data and therefore compete with the original kernel for register resources. This competition can incur substantial overhead in GPU kernels which commonly use registers heavily. We define tracing overhead as the increase in execution time relative to the original uninstrumented kernel.\nL78: As shown in Figure cite66†1(b) , Neutrino and IKET incur 11.8–75.6% and 3.8–28.4% tracing overhead respectively across the same three production kernels. Such overhead can disrupt instruction overlap and pipeline timing, causing the trace to deviate from the original execution.\nL79:    Tracing Method Project Instrumentation Level cite67†Image: [Uncaptioned image] NVIDIA cite68†Image: [Uncaptioned image] AMD Source-level instrumentation nano-trace [cite62†2 ] CUDA – FlashInfer [cite63†79 ] CUDA – IR-level instrumentation CUDAAdvisor [cite69†65 ] LLVM IR LLVM IR LOProfiler [cite70†23 ] LLVM IR LLVM IR KPerfIR [cite64†31 , cite71†42 , cite72†41 ] LLVM IR LLVM IR eGPU [cite73†73 , cite74†78 ] PTX – Neutrino [cite40†36 ] PTX RDNA Reserve-based instrumentation CuTeDSL IKET [cite41†57 ] SASS – Non-inlined instrumentation SASSI [cite75†66 ] SASS – NVBit [cite76†69 , cite77†47 ] SASS – Binary-level splicing Xtrace (Ours) SASS CDNA & RDNA\nL80: Table 1. Existing GPU intra-kernel tracing tools by tracing method and per-vendor instrumentation level. FE and BE are the frontend and the backend compilers.\nL81: These observations motivate us to seek a tracing method that preserves compiler optimizations while minimizing runtime overhead. We identify binary-level instrumentation as a promising approach, inserting probe code only after the kernel has been compiled into a binary. First, this avoids interference with compilation and leaves the compiler’s optimizations intact. Second, static analysis of the binary can identify idle registers in the regions to be probed.\nL82: Reusing these registers for profiling data avoids competition with the original kernel for register resources. Our investigation shows that such reuse opportunities are common across the GPU kernels we examine.\nL83: cite78†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2. Comparison of the instrumentation mechanisms of existing IR-level [cite40†36 ] and binary-level [cite41†57 , cite75†66 , cite76†69 ] intra-kernel tracing tools against Xtrace. FE and BE denote the frontend and backend GPU kernel compilers.\nL84: Based on this insight, we present Xtrace, a high-fidelity GPU tracing system built around instruction splicing. For each probe location, Xtrace first identifies registers that are dead at that point, then inserts the probe instructions into the surrounding instruction stream while resolving data and structural hazards under the GPU scheduling constraints.\nL85: It further arranges probe instructions to minimize new dependencies on the original kernel, allowing the probes to be integrated into the existing schedule with minimal perturbation. Together, we preserve the original instruction behavior and minimize runtime overhead, enabling high-fidelity tracing. This paper makes the following contributions:\nL86:   * •\nL87: \nL88: We identify and quantify two sources of fidelity loss in existing GPU tracing tools: compile-time interference caused by instrumentation and runtime perturbation introduced by probes. We demonstrate both effects on representative production LLM kernels.\nL89: \nL90:   * •\nL91: We propose instruction splicing, which inserts probes directly into compiled GPU binaries by exploiting available register and scheduling slack. Xtrace uses register liveness analysis and hazard-aware scheduling to preserve correctness while minimizing probe overhead.\nL92: \nL93:   * •\nL94: We implement Xtrace as a general GPU binary tracing method across 19 NVIDIA and AMD architectures and validate its high fidelity on six representative kernels. Xtrace leaves every basic block of the compiled kernel intact and preserves 94–98% of its instructions under every tracing workload. It adds only 0.9–2.8% overhead, against 3.8–75.6% for existing tools, without adding any register to the kernel. Xtrace is publicly available for use at cite39†https://g-watch.github.io†g-watch.github.io .\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL97: We show that high-fidelity tracing significantly benefits agentic kernel optimization. Xtrace guides an agent to reach the same FA-3 performance in 3.9$\\times$ fewer iterations than the best existing tracing tool, and enables trace-guided optimization of FA-4 against closed-source cuDNN, lifting its throughput by 5.2–13.3%.\nL98: ## 2. Background & Motivation\nL99: ### 2.1. GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing\nL100: High-performance GPU kernels [cite51†25 , cite79†71 , cite80†32 , cite81†20 ] tend to fuse more work into a single kernel launch to saturate GPU utilization and cut CPU-side launch overhead. These kernels such as FA-3 [cite47†64 ] build complex software pipelines to exploit both compute and memory bandwidth. Therefore, their monolithic execution becomes harder to profile with traditional counter-based profilers [cite59†61 , cite60†56 , cite61†12 ].\nL101: To regain this visibility, intra-kernel tracing captures the behavior at a finer granularity than hardware counters. As shown in Figure cite82†3 , it inserts probe code into the kernel to record the runtime state such as timestamps, and runs the probed kernel to collect the state the probes record, generating traces as shown in Figure cite83†4 (b).\nL102: Existing tools insert probes at different levels of the GPU kernel compilation stack, from source [cite62†2 , cite63†79 ] to intermediate representation (IR) [cite69†65 , cite70†23 , cite64†31 , cite71†42 , cite72†41 , cite73†73 , cite74†78 , cite40†36 ] to binary [cite75†66 , cite76†69 , cite41†57 ], as Table cite84†1 lists.\nL103: Figure 3. Example: probing FA-3 [cite47†64 ] kernel for tracing its SoftMax and P-convert region, using CuTeDSL IKET [cite41†57 ].\nL104: marks the inserted probe code.\nL105: \nL106: (a) Compiled instruction sequence.\nL107: \nL108:   X\nL109: \nL110: (b) Runtime tracing result.\nL111: Figure 4. Existing tracing tools [cite40†36 , cite41†57 ] change the FA-3 [cite47†64 ] kernel they observe on an NVIDIA H100 GPU. In (a), ➀ to ➂ mark three compiler optimizations lost under probing (see §cite9†2.2 ). In (b), each trace covers the life cycle of one cooperative thread array (CTA), which loads a single Q tile and iterates over all K/V tiles in the LLM context. to magnify four traced regions.\nL112: ### 2.2. Existing Tools Incur Significant Fidelity Loss\nL113: A trace has high fidelity only when tracing does not change the state it aims to record. Unfortunately, we find that existing tools produce low-fidelity traces that mislead developers. Prior work [cite40†36 , cite64†31 ] evaluates the fidelity of tracing by the end-to-end slowdown of the probed kernel. However, this metric is a poor indicator for two reasons.\nL114: First, the probe inserted by these tools breaks the compiler optimizations present in the original kernel, so they actually observe a different kernel even at a negligible slowdown. Second, the probes add overhead to exactly the pipeline phases that tracing aims to observe, which the end-to-end duration can hide. Below, we first demonstrate these failures, then analyze the mechanism behind them to guide our design.\nL115: We examine two state-of-the-art tracing tools, Neutrino [cite40†36 ] and IKET [cite41†57 ] (NVIDIA’s official tracing tool). Both tools instrument the kernel with the probe before compilation, so the compiler compiles the probe together with the original kernel code.\nL116: Neutrino (Figure cite85†2 a) directly inserts the probe into the kernel at the IR level, while IKET (Figure cite85†2 b) inserts placeholders that reserve resources during compilation and dynamically replaces them with the probe instructions during tracing. With both tools, we trace FA-3 on H100 to observe its software pipeline. FA-3 is a warp-specialized kernel, where a producer warp loads Q, K, and V tiles from device memory and consumer warps compute the attention.\nL117: Figure cite83†4 shows the trace each tool produces, and both tools reflect the same bottleneck: the producer warp waits on the K and V buffers released by the consumer warps for 48–52% of its time each, while the consumer warps spend 72–78% of theirs in the SoftMax. Meanwhile, Neutrino and IKET increase the kernel’s execution time by 13.0% and 5.3% respectively.\nL118: Probing breaks compiler optimizations.  However, we find that the instruction sequences of the probed kernels behind both traces significantly differ from the original’s, as the inserted probes prevent the compiler optimizations that schedule instructions across region boundaries. Figure cite86†4(a) marks three optimizations lost in the FA-3 consumer warp (➀–➂).\nL119: In the unprobed kernel, the compiler interleaves the two unrolled SoftMax iterations (➀) and overlaps P-convert with SoftMax (➁) to hide data-dependency waits. Probing prevents both optimizations, leaving two sequential SoftMax passes and a separate P-convert phase, as shown in and of Figure cite83†4 (b). The tools therefore report an inflated SoftMax time of 992 ns.\nL120: In the unprobed kernel, the compiler also advances the release of the V tile into the SoftMax region (➂), letting the producer stop waiting for it sooner. Probing leaves the release after the SoftMax, so the producer keeps waiting for the V tile until the consumer reaches the release ( and ), and the tools thus report an inflated V-tile wait of 1,024–1,216 ns.\nL121: Probing adds runtime overhead.  Besides breaking compiler optimizations, the probes also make the kernel wait at runtime. We compare the probed FA-3 against the unprobed one with the vendor’s program counter (PC) sampling tool [cite87†55 ], and Figure cite88†5 breaks down the waits around probes in both producer and consumer warps. First, both the probe and kernel instructions wait on the probe’s trace store due to Write-After-Read (WAR) register dependencies.\nL122: In the producer warp, the store at 0x0fe0 writes the trace record in R4:R5 to device memory, and the probe’s own PRMT at 0x0ff0 overwrites R5, so it waits for the store 77% of the time ().\nL123: Figure 5. Wait distribution around Neutrino’s [cite40†36 ] probes.\nL124: In the consumer warp, the store at 0x7990 holds the kernel’s first SoftMax instruction at 0x79b0 on R12, and this scoreboard wait now makes up 13% of the instruction’s waits (), none of which the unprobed kernel shows. Waiting for a store is expensive, as a global memory access takes 270 to 380 ns on Hopper GPUs [cite89†45 , cite90†40 , cite91†39 ], comparable to the traced regions in Figure cite83†4 (b) that last a few hundred nanoseconds.\nL125: Furthermore, the wait for the trace store holds the whole warp rather than the storing thread alone. Although only one thread of a warp writes the trace record, the other threads also wait for it at the reconvergence point after the probe. In Figure cite88†5 , warp synchronization thus dominates the waits of the first kernel instruction after the probe, ULEA at 0x1020 in the producer warp () and VIMNMX at 0x79b0 in the consumer warp (), and never appears in the unprobed kernel.\nL126: ### 2.3. Opportunities and Challenges\nL127: Our insights. Both failures in §cite9†2.2 stem from treating the probe as part of the kernel at compile time and at runtime. To avoid compile-time interference, another set of binary-level tools including NVBit [cite76†69 , cite77†47 ] injects a call to a probe function into the compiled binary (Figure cite85†2 c). The probe therefore runs as a separate function at each boundary of the traced region, and never enters the kernel’s own instruction sequence (non-inlined instrumentation).\nL128: However, saving and restoring the register context around each call is so costly that these tools are impractical for performance tracing. Instead, our insight is to insert the probe instructions directly into the kernel’s original instruction sequence at the binary level (Figure cite85†2 d). Although the compiled kernel binary makes no provision for the probe, the probe’s few instructions can still reuse the kernel’s registers while leaving the remaining instructions untouched.\nL129: Challenges.  However, inserting the probe into the kernel at the binary level raises three challenges for preserving the kernel’s behavior. First, the probe may overwrite a register that still holds a live value. We must identify which registers can be correctly reused at the insertion address without the compiler’s help (C1). Second, probe insertion may break the kernel’s instruction schedule and even corrupt the kernel.\nL130: The compiled binary carries explicit control information such as stall counts and dependency barriers to resolve the structural and data hazards among the kernel’s instructions (see §cite13†4.1 ). Inserting instructions changes this timing and introduces new hazards between the probe and the kernel, which we must resolve from the binary alone while preserving the original schedule (C2). Third, a correctly inserted probe still introduces overhead into the kernel’s execution.\nL131: Reusing the kernel’s registers introduces register dependencies between the probe and the kernel and thus causes dependency waits. We must keep these waits small to retain the fidelity of the observed software pipeline (C3).\nL132: ## 3. Xtrace\nL133: Overview. Xtrace aims for high-fidelity intra-kernel tracing.\nL134: To address the challenges of inserting probes at the binary level (§cite10†2.3 ), Xtrace analyzes which registers and scheduling resources are available at the insertion address and derives a correct and efficient insertion through optimization, which we call splicing.^{2}^{2} 2 We borrow the term splicing from CPU dynamic instrumentation [cite92†48 , cite93†34 , cite94†67 ], which inserts code into a compiled binary at the granularity of individual CPU instructions.\nL135: To reuse registers correctly (C1), Xtrace analyzes register liveness and assigns the probe only registers that are dead at the insertion address. Notably, the analysis covers complex cases such as warp-specialized kernels that reallocate registers at runtime and instructions that hold registers asynchronously (e.g., NVIDIA Tensor Core). To resolve instruction hazards (C2), Xtrace models GPU instruction execution and resolves the probe’s hazards with the same hazard-resolution tables as the compiler.\nL136: To reduce probe overhead (C3), Xtrace minimizes dependency waits by estimating the wait cost of each candidate splicing scheme and keeping the cheapest.\nL137: System components and workflow. Xtrace has three components as shown in Figure cite95†6 : the probe library provides probe templates for common tracing cases, which the splicer (❷) statically splices into the binary at positions matching the trace hints (❶) developers place in the kernel source code. The runtime trace driver (❸) then intercepts the kernel launch to run and trace the probed kernel.\nL138: cite96†Image: Refer to caption Figure 6. The system architecture of Xtrace, comprising the probe library, the splicer, and the runtime trace driver.\nL139: After the kernel is compiled as usual, the splicer locates the trace hints in the binary through the DWARF debug information [cite97†27 , cite98†53 , cite99†54 , cite100†10 , cite101†11 ]. It then disassembles the binary and splices the probe instructions into the instruction sequence with the analysis described above. Since DWARF does not always preserve the source-to-binary mapping, Xtrace also provides a low-level interface to splice a given probe template at a specified program address in the binary.\nL140: §cite12†4 details the splicing mechanism, and §cite18†5 presents the platforms Xtrace supports.\nL141: The driver executes the application to collect the probe’s trace. The probed kernel writes its trace to a device buffer whose size depends on the kernel’s launch configuration, known only at runtime. Xtrace therefore hijacks the kernel launch to read its parameters, and allocates a trace buffer accordingly before running the probed kernel in place of the original. Once the kernel finishes, Xtrace parses the trace results from the buffer. §cite17†4.5 details the driver.\nL142: ## 4. Splicing Probe Instructions\nL143: ### 4.1. Modeling\nL144: Figure 7. How a modern GPU schedules instructions. (a) The compiler reads structural and data hazard tables and bakes the resulting schedule into the binary. (b) A GPU core fetches, schedules, and dispatches each instruction to a pipe. (c) At issue, a per-warp stall counter and scoreboard hold each instruction until its hazards clear. The schedule resolves a structural hazard (d) and a static data hazard (e) with stalls, and a dynamic data hazard (f) with a scoreboard.\nL145: cite102†Image: Refer to caption Figure 8. Splicing a memory-access tracer probe (instructions $\\texttt{i}^{(1)}$–$\\texttt{i}^{(7)}$) into the kernel’s instruction sequence. CS2R and S2R read a special register, IMAD is an integer multiply-add, and STG stores to device memory.  marks an input of the splicing problem and  marks a decision. The registers $R$ and the scoreboards $\\mathcal{S}$ at $\\texttt{i}^{(p)}$ are each free () or held ().\nL146: GPU execution model. Modern GPUs issue their instructions in order, and the compiler resolves their hazards ahead of time and encodes the resulting schedule as control bits in each compiled instruction [cite103†37 ], as shown in Figure cite104†7 . These control bits clear three classes of hazard. First, a structural hazard between two instructions sharing a pipe is cleared by a stall that spaces their issue by the cycles that the earlier one holds the pipe, as shown in Figure cite104†7 d.\nL147: The remaining two are data hazards, which arise when two instructions access the same register. A static data hazard on a fixed-latency dependency is cleared by a stall sized to the producer’s latency, after which the consumer can issue, as shown in Figure cite104†7 e. A dynamic data hazard on a variable-latency producer, such as a memory load whose completion the compiler cannot predict, is cleared by a scoreboard the consumer waits on, as shown in Figure cite104†7 f.\nL148: A warp carries a finite set of scoreboards, which the compiler assigns across the kernel’s variable-latency dependencies. Besides the control bits, the compiler also decides the register each instruction reads and writes. Each thread has a finite register file.\nL149: Inputs and decisions. We demonstrate splicing via a memory-access tracer probe of seven instructions, shown in Figure cite105†8 . The probe records a timestamp (❶), computes this thread’s slot in a trace buffer in device memory (❷), and stores the timestamp together with the accessed address into that slot (❸). The splicer takes the probe’s instructions $I=\\{\\texttt{i}^{(1)},\\ldots,\\texttt{i}^{(7)}\\}$ together with their data dependencies $E_{\\mathrm{RAW}}$, i.e., Read-After-Write (RAW) dependencies.\nL150: For example, the store $\\texttt{i}^{(6)}$ reads the timestamp that $\\texttt{i}^{(1)}$ records. Given the insertion address $\\texttt{i}^{(p)}$, Xtrace inserts the probe ahead of it. Specifically, the splicer makes the following three decisions, which together form a splicing.\nL151:   * •\nL152: \nL153: D1 | Register allocation. The splicer assigns each value v a register $\\rho(\\texttt{v})$ that is dead at the insertion address.\nL154: \nL155:   * •\nL156: \nL157: D2 | Instruction order. The splicer picks an order $\\pi=\\langle\\texttt{i}^{(1)},\\ldots,\\texttt{i}^{(7)}\\rangle$ that respects the data dependencies $E_{\\mathrm{RAW}}$.\nL158: \nL159:   * •\nL160: \nL161: D3 | Control bits. The splicer writes the stalls and scoreboards $\\gamma$ that resolve the three classes of hazard.\nL162: Enumerating a probe’s decision space. During the compilation of GPU kernels, the compiler makes the three decisions in separate passes. A kernel has thousands of instructions and up to hundreds of available registers, so its joint decision space is too large to enumerate [cite106†30 , cite107†13 ]. Each pass therefore decides with heuristics instead [cite108†72 , cite109†44 ].\nL163: However, a probe leaves a far smaller space, as the insertion address offers few dead registers, the probe’s several instructions admit few orders, and their hazards permit few control-bit settings. This gives Xtrace the opportunity to enumerate that space and keep the cheapest splicing in it. §cite14†4.2 defines that cost and gives the search algorithm. The search rests on two analyses of the kernel binary, the register liveness of §cite15†4.3 and the hazard resolution of §cite16†4.4 .\nL164: ### 4.2. The Splicing Algorithm\nL165: Probe overhead. A splicing fixes the earliest issue time of every instruction once the control bits have resolved its hazards. Xtrace evaluates the overhead of a splicing at the insertion address $\\texttt{i}^{(p)}$ as $o=t[\\texttt{i}^{(p)}]-t_{0}[\\texttt{i}^{(p)}]$, where $t$ is the issue time under the spliced schedule and $t_{0}$ the issue time under the unprobed one.\nL166: However, $o$ undercounts the cost of a dynamic data hazard, which blocks the consumer instruction at runtime for a latency that cannot be predicted deterministically. Xtrace therefore corrects the overhead into $o^{\\prime}$ by adding a measured latency for each dynamic data dependency:\nL167: (1)  |  | $$o^{\\prime}=t^{\\prime}[\\texttt{i}^{(p)}]-t_{0}[\\texttt{i}^{(p)}],$$  |\nL168: (2)  |  | $$t^{\\prime}[\\texttt{i}^{(k)}]=\\max_{\\texttt{i}^{(j)}\\to\\texttt{i}^{(k)}}\\big(t^{\\prime}[\\texttt{i}^{(j)}]+\\delta[\\texttt{i}^{(j)}{\\to}\\texttt{i}^{(k)}]\\big),$$  |\nL169: where $\\texttt{i}^{(j)}$ ranges over the instructions preceding $\\texttt{i}^{(k)}$ in the instruction order, and the spacing $\\delta$ combines the stalls for resolving static hazards with the measured-latency correction. We microbenchmark the completion latencies of common dynamic-hazard instructions on mainstream GPUs, and Xtrace hardcodes a conservative value from each in the correction.\nL170: Joint search for the cheapest splicing. Xtrace computes a splicing with Algorithm cite110†1 , which searches the three decisions jointly. Before the search, Xtrace analyzes the kernel binary for its register liveness and control bits (step 1), detailed in §cite15†4.3 and §cite16†4.4 . In the main search loop, Xtrace enumerates the instruction orders of the probe (step 2).\nL171: An order grows one instruction at a time, and an instruction is eligible once every producer it depends on in $E_{\\mathrm{RAW}}$ has been emitted. For each order, Xtrace enumerates the register assignments over the dead registers and the control bits the hazard tables permit (steps 3–4). Xtrace evaluates each valid plan by its corrected overhead $o^{\\prime}$ and keeps the cheapest one (steps 5–8).\nL172: Algorithm 1 The optimal splicing algorithm.\nL173: \nL174: 1: kernel binary $K$, insertion address $\\texttt{i}^{(p)}$ probe instructions $I$ with data dependencies $E_{\\mathrm{RAW}}$\nL175: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKREX: Concurrent Kernel Benchmarking on Shared GPUs via Region-Granular Exclusivity (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30057v1)\nciteturn36view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30057v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 331\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - SyFI-ServingStudio/ServingStudio · GitHub (https://github.com/SyFI-ServingStudio/ServingStudio)\nciteturn36view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/SyFI-ServingStudio/ServingStudio\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 380\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn36view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://syfi-servingstudio.github.io/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"
